00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.900
Mind Your Decisions, Presh Talwalkar here.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:17.840
The American Invitational Mathematics Examination, or AIME for short, is a qualifying test in America for the International Mathematical Olympiad. It's a 3-hour test that includes 15 questions, and

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.420
calculators are not allowed.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:25.080
Every answer is a whole number from 0 to 999

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:31.019
inclusive. In this video, we'll cover problem 7 from the 1984 exam:

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.759
"The function f is defined on the integers and

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:43.500
satisfies f(n)=n-3 if n ⩾ 1000, and

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:49.680
it's equal to f(f(n+5)) if n &lt; 1000.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.580
The question is: what is f(84)?

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.620
I thank Henry from the Philippines for suggesting this problem.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:04.680
Pause the video if you'd like to give it a try, and when you're ready, keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:22.740
So if you're like me, you try to solve this problem by directly calculating f(84).

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:28.520
Since 84 &lt; 1000, this is equal to f(f(89)).

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:35.380
Since 89 &lt; 1,000, this is equal to f(f(f(94))).

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:43.360
If you try working this direction a little further, it feels like you're trying to open a Russian doll:

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.500
each time you're trying to simplify the problem, you end up with a copy of the problem, and

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.320
it doesn't seem to be getting any simpler. It just seems to be getting more and more nested and,

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.300
eventually, you're not getting anywhere

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:04.740
So sometimes, when you try and solve a problem, you end up with a dead-end but don't despair.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.140
Try another approach.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:12.520
Rather than directly trying to calculate f(84), let's try working from the other direction.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:18.700
Let's try calculating for values around 1,000. What is f(999)?

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:24.060
By definition, this is equal to f(f(1004)).

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:30.809
Now, by definition, f(1004) is equal to n-3, which is 1001.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:36.720
Now, we apply the definition once more , and we get the answer of 998.

00:02:38.109 --> 00:02:41.429
We'll do a similar thing to calculate f(998).

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:46.840
We applied the definition of f(n), and we end up with 997.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.120
Let's try working out a few more examples. What's f(997)?

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:57.869
While we work through the definition of f(n), we get to f(999).

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.180
Luckily, we've just calculated that this is equal to 998

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.720
We can do a similar calculation for f(996),

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:13.340
where we get to f(998), and this is equal to 997.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:26.440
Let's calculate a few more: what's f(995)? We work through the definition and we get to f(997), which is equal to 988.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:33.900
f(994) will be equal to f(f(999)), or will be equal to f(998), and that's equal to 997.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.780
So we see a pattern emerging:

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.160
for values less than

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.900
1,000, f(odd number) = 998,

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:49.040
998, and f(even number) = 997.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.220
You can prove this by induction.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:58.880
Ultimately, we get that f(84), since 84 is an even number, will be equal to

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:02.080
997, and that's our answer.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.520
Now, I just want to do a little extension for fun.

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:12.279
This problem can actually be solved very elegantly and very simply using computer code.

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:21.160
Now, I know it's a problem from a 1984 exam, but we can actually use modern computer code, like Python, to solve this very quickly.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:25.750
So we wanted to find a function doll(n).

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:31.900
If n is &gt;= 1000, we want to get the value of n-3.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.540
Otherwise, we want to get the value of f(f(n+5)).

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:46.180
This will be written as return doll(doll(n+5)),and this is something pretty interesting.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:50.860
We're defining the function doll in terms of the function doll!

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:55.500
This is an example of recursion, and we'll use it to solve this problem now

00:04:56.660 --> 00:05:06.680
You just type in this code, and then type in print(str(doll(84))), and you'll get the answer of 997 instantly!

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:14.420
it's amazing that Python can solve this problem so quickly, and all you need to do is type in the definition of this problem.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:21.160
So this is a great problem from a 1984 test, and the problem can illustrate the power of computer code!

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:31.060
